---
title: "Serve agents on other hosts with mutual TLS"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Serve agents on other hosts with mutual TLS}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("tools/guides/setup.R")
```

You want agents on other hosts or accounts to share one endpoint without
letting any of them pose as another. On `ipc://`, filesystem permissions
decide who may connect; on a network listener, "who is calling?" needs a
stronger answer than the agent ID a client types. On mutual TLS the endpoint
answers it with the client's certificate, and a grants file decides which
certificate may act as which agent.

## Certificates

A throwaway CA signs a server certificate for `127.0.0.1` and client
certificates for three identities. In production these come from your own
PKI:

```{bash}
install -d -m 700 "$STATE/tls" && cd "$STATE/tls"
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout ca.key -out ca.pem -days 1 \
  -subj /CN=guide-ca 2> /dev/null
issue() {
  openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout "$1.key" -out "$1.csr" -subj "/CN=$2" 2> /dev/null
  printf '%b\n' "$3" > "$1.ext"
  openssl x509 -req -in "$1.csr" -CA ca.pem -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial \
    -out "$1.crt" -days 1 -extfile "$1.ext" 2> /dev/null
  cat "$1.crt" "$1.key" > "$1.pem"
}
issue server 127.0.0.1 'subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1\nextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth'
for client in lead reviewer intruder; do issue "$client" "$client" 'extendedKeyUsage=clientAuth'; done
ls server.pem lead.pem reviewer.pem intruder.pem
```

## Grants and the listener

The identity ducknng verifies has the form `tls:cn:<common name>`. The grants
file allows `lead` to act as any agent in `guide`, and `reviewer` only as
`reviewer`:

```{bash}
cat > "$STATE/grants.json" <<'JSON'
[{"peer_identity": "tls:cn:lead", "project_id": "guide", "agent_id": "*"},
 {"peer_identity": "tls:cn:reviewer", "project_id": "guide", "agent_id": "reviewer"}]
JSON
```

The endpoint takes its certificate and key, the client CA, and the grants file
as options. The same certificate secures the wake-up hint listener, which here
uses `wss://`:

```{bash}
node tools/pi-coordination-endpoint.ts "$STATE/mtls.url" "$STATE/mtls.duckdb" \
  tls+tcp://127.0.0.1:0 \
  --tls-cert-key "$STATE/tls/server.pem" --tls-ca "$STATE/tls/ca.pem" \
  --grants "$STATE/grants.json" --events wss://127.0.0.1:0/events \
  > "$STATE/mtls.log" 2>&1 &
echo $! > "$STATE/mtls.pid"
until [ -s "$STATE/mtls.url" ]; do sleep 0.1; done
cat "$STATE/mtls.url" | sed 's/:[0-9]*$/:<port>/'
```

## Calling as a certificate

A Pi session names its CA and its own certificate file in
`PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CA_FILE` and `PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CERT_KEY_FILE`.
`tools/coordination-call.ts` reads the same variables, so a small shell helper
can call any method as any of the three identities:

```{bash}
cat > "$STATE/as.sh" <<'SH'
as() {
  identity="$1"; shift
  PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CA_FILE="$STATE/tls/ca.pem" \
  PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CERT_KEY_FILE="$STATE/tls/$identity.pem" \
    node tools/coordination-call.ts "$(cat "$STATE/mtls.url")" "$@" 2>&1
}
register() {
  as "$1" register '{"project_id": "'"$3"'", "agent_id": "'"$2"'",
    "instance_id": "'"$1-$2"'", "operation_key": "guide"}' "${4:-agent_id}"
}
SH
. "$STATE/as.sh"
register lead planner guide
register lead reviewer guide
register reviewer reviewer guide
```

Everything outside the grants is refused before any state changes:

```{bash}
. "$STATE/as.sh"
register reviewer planner guide || true
register reviewer reviewer other-project || true
register intruder reviewer guide || true
```

## A registration belongs to its certificate

Registration IDs are opaque, but they are not bearer tokens. A registration
works only for the certificate that created it, and an instance cannot be
taken over by another certificate, even one granted the same agent:

```{bash}
. "$STATE/as.sh"
id=$(register reviewer reviewer guide registration_id |
  node -pe 'JSON.parse(require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8")).registration_id')
as reviewer list_agents '{"registration_id": "'"$id"'"}' agents.length
as lead list_agents '{"registration_id": "'"$id"'"}' || true
as lead register '{"project_id": "guide", "agent_id": "reviewer",
  "instance_id": "reviewer-reviewer", "operation_key": "takeover"}' || true
```

## Wake-up hints over wss

`register` also returns the hint listener and an opaque topic for the
mailbox. A subscriber presenting a certificate wakes as soon as mail lands:

```{bash}
PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CA_FILE="$STATE/tls/ca.pem" \
PI_DUCKNNG_TLS_CERT_KEY_FILE="$STATE/tls/lead.pem" \
URL="$(cat "$STATE/mtls.url")" node --input-type=module -e '
const { ducknngCoordinationClient: client, subscribeCoordinationEvents } =
  await import("./extensions/pi-ducknng/index.ts");
const url = process.env.URL;
await client.describe(url);
const join = (agent) => client.call(url, "register", {
  project_id: "guide", agent_id: agent, instance_id: `hint-${agent}`, operation_key: "hint" });
const planner = await join("planner");
const worker = await join("worker");
let woke = 0;
const subscription = await subscribeCoordinationEvents(
  worker.events.url, worker.events.topic, () => { woke = Date.now(); });
await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 300));
const sent = Date.now();
await client.call(url, "send", { registration_id: planner.registration_id,
  recipient_agent_id: "worker", idempotency_key: "wake", content: "new work" });
while (!woke && Date.now() - sent < 3000) await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 5));
console.log(`hint listener: ${worker.events.url.replace(/:\d+/, ":<port>")}`);
console.log(`woke ${woke ? "within 1 s: " + (woke - sent < 1000) : "never"}`);
await subscription.close();
process.exit(0);'
```

The hint carries only the topic, so a subscriber learns that mail arrived
and nothing else. The mail itself still travels through `receive`.

```{bash}
#| include: false
kill "$(cat "$STATE/mtls.pid")"
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat("> GUIDE_MTLS_VERIFIED: grants admit only the named certificates, registrations stay with their certificate, and hints arrive over wss\n")
```
